    "id": "healey-to-shield-low-income-pensioners-f-53d6abfa",
    "title": "Healey to shield low-income pensioners from income tax in the Budget",
    "titleJa": "Healey to shield low-income pensioners from income tax in the Budget",
    "summaryJa": "Changes are needed to prevent the full, flat-rate state pension becoming liable for income tax after it rises in April.",
    "bodyOriginal": "Healey to shield low-income pensioners from income tax in the Budget\n- Published\nPensioners on low incomes will be shielded from income tax by Chancellor John Healey in his first Budget on Wednesday.\nThe full, flat-rate state pension is expected to increase by 3.9% to £13,036.40 a year from April. This would take the payment above the £12,570 personal tax allowance and therefore be liable for income tax if no changes are made.\nThe government had already committed to not taxing people who only get an income from the state pension. Healey will explain this pledge will be met by plans for a new tax allowance for low-income pensioners.\nHealey's plans come as the Conservatives and Reform UK unveil their alternative Budget proposals, including deep cuts to the welfare bill.\nWhat is the Budget and what could be in it?\n- Published10 hours ago\nAn insider's guide to Budget Day: 'Don't fall off the kerb'\n- Published6 hours ago\nThe chancellor's allies argue his plans for pensioners will mean they do not need to face an administrative burden just to pay a small amount of tax.\nMore specific details, including how many pensioners this will affect, have yet to be confirmed by the Treasury.\nThe Budget is due to be printed this evening, with Healey's speech likely to be fine-tuned right up until it is delivered on Wednesday lunchtime.\nTreasury sources concede it will not involve the scale of tax and spending announcements seen from Labour and Conservative governments in recent years.\nThe Tories are calling on the chancellor to cut green taxes, red tape and benefits to bring down energy bills.\nReform is recommending the chancellor adopts 60 policies which they claim will create jobs - including approving the controversial Rosebank oil field - a project Healey is understood to back.\nLiberal Democrat Treasury spokeswoman Daisy Cooper has called on the government to agree a new growth and defence partnership with the EU, including joining the single market. She argued this would help \"ease the huge tax burden weighing people down\".\nThe Green Party has urged Healey to use his first Budget to \"make the UK affordable\", including by funding energy bill support by ending a tax relief for data centres.\nSenior Green politicians, including leader Zack Polanski, MPs and peers, made clear their suggestions in a letter to Healey.\nSign up for our Politics Essential newsletter to keep up with the inner workings of Westminster and beyond.",
    "bodyJa": "Healey to shield low-income pensioners from income tax in the Budget\n- Published\nPensioners on low incomes will be shielded from income tax by Chancellor John Healey in his first Budget on Wednesday.\nThe full, flat-rate state pension is expected to increase by 3.9% to £13,036.40 a year from April. This would take the payment above the £12,570 personal tax allowance and therefore be liable for income tax if no changes are made.\nThe government had already committed to not taxing people who only get an income from the state pension. Healey will explain this pledge will be met by plans for a new tax allowance for low-income pensioners.\nHealey's plans come as the Conservatives and Reform UK unveil their alternative Budget proposals, including deep cuts to the welfare bill.\nWhat is the Budget and what could be in it?\n- Published10 hours ago\nAn insider's guide to Budget Day: 'Don't fall off the kerb'\n- Published6 hours ago\nThe chancellor's allies argue his plans for pensioners will mean they do not need to face an administrative burden just to pay a small amount of tax.\nMore specific details, including how many pensioners this will affect, have yet to be confirmed by the Treasury.\nThe Budget is due to be printed this evening, with Healey's speech likely to be fine-tuned right up until it is delivered on Wednesday lunchtime.\nTreasury sources concede it will not involve the scale of tax and spending announcements seen from Labour and Conservative governments in recent years.\nThe Tories are calling on the chancellor to cut green taxes, red tape and benefits to bring down energy bills.\nReform is recommending the chancellor adopts 60 policies which they claim will create jobs - including approving the controversial Rosebank oil field - a project Healey is understood to back.\nLiberal Democrat Treasury spokeswoman Daisy Cooper has called on the government to agree a new growth and defence partnership with the EU, including joining the single market. She argued this would help \"ease the huge tax burden weighing people down\".\nThe Green Party has urged Healey to use his first Budget to \"make the UK affordable\", including by funding energy bill support by ending a tax relief for data centres.\nSenior Green politicians, including leader Zack Polanski, MPs and peers, made clear their suggestions in a letter to Healey.\nSign up for our Politics Essential newsletter to keep up with the inner workings of Westminster and beyond.",
    "source": "BBC",
    "sourceUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ck98zp7zeq65o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
    "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T10:28:12+00:00",
    "category": "エネルギー",
    "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/standard/240/cpsprodpb/1d71/live/21ebf010-c606-11f1-a64c-550be9e3c66b.jpg",
